---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Figures from "../../../components/Figures.astro";
import Series from "../../../components/Series.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Visualization — Lagom"
  description="Charts for Lagom are planned as static files. None are published yet."
  canonical="https://ofantini.com/projects/lagom/visualization"
  current="projects"
  inventory="platform"
  figurePage="visualization"
>
  <article class="essay">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Lagom</p>
      <h1>No chart is published yet.</h1>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A visitor must not reach the cluster, and a dashboard server would sit in memory the rest
          of the time. The decision is static files. Evidence is the tool chosen for the charts:
          SQL and Markdown, built by a Dagster step after each run, then published. That build does
          not exist yet. There is no dashboard on this site, and no number of charts to invent.
        </p>
        <p>
          This personal site is the pages you are reading. Publishing them to the domain is still
          open. The gallery of showcases is the other half of the same milestone. Grafana, later,
          would be for the operator, not for this public site, and only after the earthquake
          showcase is live. Streamlit waits until an interactive app cannot be a static page.
        </p>
        <p>
          What the architecture does record is the memory the public site is allowed to use on the
          node, and the fact that the gallery is not this set of essays.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figures page="visualization" />
      <Series here="/projects/lagom/visualization" />
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
